ernibles property, `zero_iff_eq_prop`, establishes that an edit distance of zero implies sequence equality, and this has to be proved both ways (implication and reverse implication). Finally, if sequences are unequal, then the edit distance is at least one, expressed by `pos_of_ne_prop`.\n\n\nDefining the symmetry property, stating that the edit distance between two sequences is the same regardless of order. Considering an upper bound property, showing that the edit distance is at most the maximum length of the input sequences. Also, formulating a property stating the edit distance is at most the sum of the input sequence lengths.\n\n\nLower bound property based on the difference in sequence lengths is being formulated, stating the edit distance must be at least the absolute difference in sequence lengths. This property, along with the single-element insertion/deletion step bounds, is now being defined. Further, I am formalizing the property that adding a common prefix to both sequences does not change their edit distance.\n\n\nFormalizing properties for the edit distance, including a recurrence specification for non-empty sequences and the triangle inequality. Also defining a subadditivity property relating to sequence concatenation. Considering how to translate the properties into corresponding theorems.\n\nAll 14 properties appear to be directly provable based on the edit distance equations and structural induction on sequence length. The proofs for base cases (`empty_left_thm`, `empty_right_thm`, `recurrence_thm`) are direct applications of the `edit_distance` definition. The reflexive property (`self_zero_thm`) can be proven using induction on the sequence. Proofs for equality (`zero_iff_eq_thm`) require induction and case analysis.\n\n\nAnalyzing proof strategies for the formalized properties. Considering how to adapt induction schemes for theorems related to the edit distance function. Specifically evaluating direct proof techniques for the reflexive and inequality properties. Now, working through the symmetry theorem proof by induction.\n\nThe proofs for base cases (`empty_left_thm`, `empty_right_thm`, `recurrence_thm`) are direct applications of the `edit_distance` definition. The reflexive property (`self_zero_thm`) can be proven using induction on the sequence. Proofs for equality (`zero_iff_eq_thm`) require induction and case analysis. The proof for the positivity of non-equal sequences (`pos_of_ne_thm`) follows directly from the equality theorem. The symmetry theorem is being approached with nested induction on sequence lengths.\n\n\nThe symmetry theorem appears provable using nested induction. The upper bound theorem uses double induction and the omega rule, but the inner induction can be simplified to a case split. The upper bound sum theorem follows directly from the upper bound max theorem. The lower bound diff theorem is provable through double induction.\n\n\nInitial attempt at proving the step bound property led to the realization that the triangle inequality could provide a more direct proof strategy. Considering how to prove the triangle inequality using strong induction on the combined length of the input sequences. The base cases for the triangle inequality appear straightforward, but the inductive step requires careful consideration of the different cases within the edit distance calculation. Tracing the proof strategy for the triangle inequality theorem by strong induction is being evaluated.\n\n\nTriangle inequality proof begins with case analysis on empty and non-empty sequences. Empty sequence cases are proven using the lower bound difference theorem. A case for `s2` being empty leverages the upper bound sum theorem. Strong induction is needed for the main inductive step, considering various character match/mismatch scenarios. Continuing analysis of the inductive step's subcases.\n\n\nConsidering how to complete the proof of the triangle inequality. Focusing on the inductive step and the possible edit operations on the sequences. Analyzing the case where the leading characters `a` and `c` are equal, determining if the distance satisfies the property. Attempting to apply the definitions to show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side. The key is in proving the base cases for all the sub-cases.\n\n\nThe inductive step's case analysis is proving crucial for the triangle inequality. Focusing on Subcase 4a, and applying the inductive hypothesis on a modified triple. Realized that the step bound theorem directly applies to the inequality from the induction. Checking whether the `step_bound_thm` is provable using existing theorems.\n\n\nRevisiting the proof strategy for the single-step bound properties. Realized that proving these four properties by strong induction on the sum of sequence lengths is crucial: `edit_distance (a :: s1) s2 <= 1 + edit_distance s1 s2`, `edit_distance s1 (a :: s2) <= 1 + edit_distance s1 s2`, `edit_distance (a :: s1) (b :: s2) <= 1 + edit_distance s1 (b :: s2)`, and `edit_distance (a :: s1) (b :: s2) <= 1 + edit_distance (a :: s1) s2`. This requires generalizing and proving all these bounds in conjunction.\n\n\nConsidering a proof strategy to establish edit distance step bounds via strong induction on the combined sequence lengths, and evaluating if the relationships among the four stated bounds are mutually provable via this approach. Beginning to trace the inductive steps. It looks like the case when the characters are equal may allow application of the `edit_distance` definition on the smaller sequences, by reducing the problem space. The key is to prove the base cases for all the sub-cases.\n\n\nThe step bound property proof now centers on strong induction on the sum of sequence lengths. Analyzing the inductive step where the leading characters `a` and `b` differ to derive the RHS bound of the inequality. The key now focuses on proving the four bounds: `edit_distance (a :: s1) s2 <= 1 + edit_distance s1 s2